   "screen": "Colour harmony rules",
   "title": "Figure 1.2.2 · Colour harmony schemes on the RYB colour wheel",
   "alt": "Six small twelve-part RYB colour wheels with red at the top, each with the hues of one scheme highlighted. Monochromatic: blue only, with its tints, shades and tones. Complementary: blue and orange, directly opposite, joined by a line. Analogous: yellow, yellow-orange and orange, side by side. Triadic: red, yellow and blue, joined in a triangle 120 degrees apart. Split-complementary: blue with red-orange and yellow-orange, the two hues either side of its complement orange, joined in a narrow triangle. Tetradic: red, green, orange and blue, two complementary pairs joined in a rectangle.",
   "caption": "Each scheme is a shape on the wheel: a line for complementary, a triangle for triadic, a narrow triangle for split-complementary and a rectangle for tetradic. Split-complementary uses three colours; tetradic uses four.",
